{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_62v7q5rwrwna2wqqd7sf5mkn4zxa6dy566xsznlphvg27vizyooq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases passed, independently re-verified on disk.\n\n## Result: the Rust translation is correct — no changes to `src/lib.rs` were needed\n\n`cargo check` was clean on the first run, and no behavioural divergence from the C was found across 53 differential tests.\n\n### Phase A — surface mapped (`SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`)\nThe C library (`c_src/src/lib.c`, 15 functions) exports exactly **one** dynamic symbol, `call_predict`; everything else is `static`. The Rust `.so` exports it too — symbol diff **empty**, 0 undefined non-libc symbols. All 15 C functions are present in the Rust source; nothing stubbed, no `unimplemented!()`.\n\nNote: `c_src/include/lib.h` declares `get_predict_func`, which `lib.c` never defines — it produces no symbol, so the Rust correctly does not export it.\n\n### Phase B — 37/37 `CONFIGS.md` rows pass\nSince `call_predict` only observes function-pointer *identity*, testing it alone would leave the predictor arithmetic (85% of the code) completely unverified. So the harness also reaches the `static` predictors across the real ABI boundary, by computing `runtime_addr(call_predict) − link_addr(call_predict) + link_addr(sym)` on **both** `.so`s. That let me differentially verify all 12 specialised predictors, the 16-case generic switch, and the FIR paths over an `idx` × `psamp` × `firfx` matrix including `i32::MIN`/`i32::MAX` wrap-around and negative operands.\n\n### Phase C — 14/14 `ERRORS.md` rows pass\nThe C has **no** error macros, asserts, null checks or range checks; its entire rejection surface is three `switch default:` fall-backs. Each is covered, asserting the same sentinel (`0`) — including out-of-range \"enum\" values (`12..=4096`, `±2^k`, `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX`, 20 000 random `i32`), which is the class happy-path tests miss.\n\n### Phase D — 53 tests × 2 feature combinations, symbol diff empty in both\n`Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]`, so `{default}` and `{--no-default-features}` is the complete set; `run_all_feature_combos.sh` enumerates them from `Cargo.toml` rather than hardcoding.\n\n### Two things worth flagging\n1. **The C contains three deliberate inconsistencies** that a translator is likely to \"fix\": `Pfn10` uses `>>3` where `case 10` uses `>>4`; `Pfn11` uses `>>1` where `case 11` uses `>>3`; and `/16`,`/64`,`/256` truncate toward zero while sibling cases use flooring `>>`. The translation reproduces all three verbatim, and `cfg_c28b_generic_vs_specialised_discrepancy_preserved` asserts the disagreement *pattern* matches C and that the divergences are actually observed — so a silent \"correction\" would fail rather than pass.\n\n2. **I mutation-tested the harness** (`mutation_check.sh`) rather than trusting green tests: 11 injected bugs, suite fails on all 11, and correctly still passes on one deliberately behaviour-preserving control mutant. This caught a flaw in my own first attempt — a mutation I had assumed was a real bug turned out to be semantically equivalent, which I reclassified instead of treating as a blind spot. The script restores `src/lib.rs` and rebuilds; I confirmed the source is pristine afterwards.\n\nOnly `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` (adding `libloading`) and new test/doc/script files were touched; `c_src/` is unmodified. One environmental note: crates.io is unreachable here, so all cargo commands use `--offline` against the pre-populated registry cache (`libloading 0.8.9`)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1235,"cache_read_input_tokens":107582,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1235,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eaf788f0-f3